WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.170
- [Instructor] Flynn's
sister loaned him some money,

00:00:02.170 --> 00:00:04.240
and he paid her back over time.

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:06.830
Flynn graphed the relationship
between how much time

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:09.505
had passed, in weeks, since
the loan and how much money

00:00:09.505 --> 00:00:11.740
he still owed his sister.

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:13.710
What feature of the graph represents

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:17.260
how long it took Flynn
to pay back the loan?

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:19.970
Pause this video, see if
you can figure that out.

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:22.960
All right, now let's go
through each of these choices.

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:25.030
So the first one is the slope.

00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:26.170
So the slope tells us

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:28.150
how much do we change in
the vertical direction

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:30.710
for any given change in
the horizontal direction.

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:32.890
So for example, if we start from here,

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:35.770
we can see that if one week goes by,

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:37.650
we go from one week to two weeks,

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:39.620
we can see that our loan went down.

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:41.930
It went from $15 to $10.

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:44.550
So when we had plus one weeks in time,

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:48.070
our loan went down by $5.

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:50.760
And then that happens over the next week.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:53.730
And that rate of how
quickly the loan is paid,

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:55.410
that's what the slope tells us.

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:57.580
And it would be constant over the course

00:00:57.580 --> 00:01:00.510
of the entire time period
is what makes this a line,

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:02.820
is that slope is always
going to be constant.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:04.600
So the slope is useful for the rate

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:06.360
at which the loan is being paid back,

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.321
but it's not the clearest
way to figure out

00:01:08.321 --> 00:01:11.040
how long it took Flynn
to pay back the loan.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:12.720
So I would rule that one out.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.410
The x-intercept, that's
where the graph intersects

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:16.690
the horizontal axis,

00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:19.072
which is often referred to as the x-axis.

00:01:19.072 --> 00:01:21.270
Or another way to think about it,

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:24.297
that tells us, that
says, what is the x-value

00:01:24.297 --> 00:01:29.150
when our vertical value, our
y-value, is equal to zero?

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:30.680
And our y-value is the money owed.

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:33.540
So it says, hey how much
time has passed when we owe,

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:34.810
when we don't owe any more money

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:37.740
or when Flynn doesn't owe
any more money to his sister?

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:39.140
Well that's exactly
what they're asking for.

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:41.170
How long did it take Flynn
to pay back the loan?

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:42.870
And that's what that x-intercept tells us.

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:44.230
It took him four weeks.

00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:45.868
After four weeks, he
didn't owe any more money

00:01:45.868 --> 00:01:47.460
to his sister.

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:49.640
So I like this choice,
but let's just review

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:50.473
the other ones.

00:01:50.473 --> 00:01:52.570
The y-intercept, well
that's where the graph

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:55.460
intersects the y-axis right over there.

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:58.670
This tells us what's going
on at time t equals zero.

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:01.690
At time t equal zero or time equal zero,

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:04.410
when the horizontal
variable is equal to zero,

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:07.020
we see that Flynn owes $20.

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:09.840
So this one isn't valuable
for figuring out when he,

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:11.280
how long it takes to pay it back.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:12.896
This one is useful for figuring out

00:02:12.896 --> 00:02:15.650
how much did he owe initially.

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:16.650
So we'll rule that one out.

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:17.890
And we already picked one choice,

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:19.740
so we can rule out none of the above.

